\IfFileExists{ou_robustness_sensitivity.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_robustness_sensitivity}
    \caption{OU--III sensitivity about the nominal operating point under the
    deployed SpectralMSE regularizer. Solid curves are OFAT perturbations;
    dashed curves follow the analytical coupling in
    Eqs.~\eqref{eq:robustness-sigma-coupling}--\eqref{eq:robustness-tau-coupling}.
    Error bars are paired-bootstrap 95\% intervals and the vertical marker is
    the nominal point.}
    \label{fig:ou_robustness_sensitivity}
  \end{figure}
}{}

The OFAT results already show the local hierarchy. Over the full multiplier
range, the direct $\sigma_{aw}$ sweep is nearly flat in vertical error, the
$\tau$ sweep moves it modestly, and the $r_S$ sweep has the largest vertical
span. This does not make $\tau$ or $\sigma_{aw}$ unused: the accelerometer
observes the latent acceleration directly at \SI{200}{Hz}, so moderate prior
rescaling has less effect on vertical displacement once that channel is
information-rich. Attitude can remain more sensitive because the
latent-acceleration covariance also enters the accelerometer innovation
covariance.

Two ten-seed degradation cases extend the main validation envelope without
changing the deployed law. The first uses a low-motion $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$
record to expose the approximately fixed absolute sensor-error floor. The
second compresses the controlled transition between the same endpoints from
\SI{120}{s} to \SI{30}{s}; Adaptive and FixedNominal are both scored so the
transition degradation remains separated from the fixed operating-point
baseline.

